  preserves the source's own words, its label, and the time it was read
  preserves the refusal; a source that said no appears on the page as having said no
  drops nothing of the item; the page shows what the contract selected and names the host it came from
  forbidden_inference an item on the wall -> a claim of this estate's
  forbidden_inference a source being read -> a source this estate may redistribute

interaction weather-schedules-observation
  source internet-census
  destination pack-graph
  relation reads
  preserves the demand exactly as an activated pack declared it
  drops installations; a held pack is not a running one and should not keep the network warm
  forbidden_inference a demanded family -> a source that will be read; a refusal still wins
  forbidden_inference a hot source -> a source anybody is looking at

architecture content-write-surface
  canonical_name ContentWriteSurface
  family surface
  domain Surface / Authoring
  layer surface
  semantic_question How does something get written into this estate, and does the surface or a shell do it?
  purpose The half of the Content Studio that only a browser could reach. /cms/v1/api/<type> had a session check, a schema check and a receipt, and answered in HTML fragments -- so an agent, a script or this estate's own tooling got markup where an answer should be, and every authoring act routed around the surface and went at the store through a shell instead. A write path with no machine projection is a write path that will be bypassed, and the session, the schema and the receipt are bypassed with it.
  maturity implemented
  source_path cmd/BonfyreWebAddress/src/main.c
  consumes Authority
  publishes ContentRecord
  internal_calculus one surface, two projections, decided by what the caller asked for. Every refusal and the save itself go through one helper that emits either the desk's fragment or the same outcome as bonfyre-cms-write.v1. The store is still written by the organ that owns it: the type must be registered, the fields must validate, and nothing here builds SQL.
  invariant one closure, two projections; a second endpoint for machines would be two ways to write one store, which is the shape the census, the packs and the patterns all exist to avoid
  invariant a refusal reads the same in both projections; a machine that is told no in markup will be read as a success by whatever parses it
  invariant the write surface is not authority to write; the session carries the scope or this refuses, and the store refuses again on its own terms
  invariant the authoring desk offers every grammar this estate registered, not the first six by name; a surface that hides the types it was built to author is a surface people go around
  cannot_infer an authored record -> authority; it says what this institution wrote, never that it is true
  cannot_infer a reachable write surface -> a permitted write
  known_gap authoring still requires a session this process does not hold, so the estate's own tooling reaches the surface and stops at the gate; the gate is right and the tooling has no service identity
  known_gap the desk generates a form from the type's columns and knows nothing about which fields are a contract -- an extraction path, a cadence, a tier -- so a census row is authorable and not guided

interaction clones-render-surface-patterns
  source market-interface-clones
  destination surface-pattern
  relation renders
  preserves the record set and its order; a clone reads /_surface/<name> and receives the same bytes a machine asking for that path receives
  preserves the refusal, in the carrier the caller asked for; a gated pattern refuses the page exactly as it refuses a machine
  drops the descriptor's own render primitives; the clone re-renders the records in a borrowed layout rather than the pattern's compiled markup
  forbidden_inference a clone's layout -> the pattern's compiled projection
  forbidden_inference a rendered pattern -> an authorized effect on its subject

architecture world-publication
  canonical_name WorldPublication
  family surface
  domain Surface / Temporary publication
  layer surface
  semantic_question When a subject gets enough public relationships to stop being a row, what does it become, and does that cost a new application?
  purpose The thing was never a card. An earthquake, a race weekend, an underground night, a tag people are posting under -- each accumulates enough live relationships that a list item is the wrong container, and each wants a different visual character: an atlas, a pit wall, a xerox flyer wall, a scrapbook. The catalogue answer is fifteen applications. This is the other answer: a world is one authored publication over one live subject, its character is a field, and the interaction it offers is the grammars the estate already built.
  maturity implemented
  source_path cmd/BonfyreCMS/content-types/world.json
  source_path cmd/BonfyreCMS/content-types/world_fragment.json
  source_path cmd/BonfyreCMS/content-types/publication_occurrence.json
  source_path schemas/control/publication-occurrence.yaff
  source_path schemas/control/publication-occurrence.cue
  source_path schemas/control/world-material-renderers.cue
  source_path cmd/BonfyreCMS/tools/materialize_world_fragments.mjs
