architecture canonical-subject-address
  canonical_name CanonicalSubjectAddress
  family surface
  domain Surface / Canonical address
  layer surface
  semantic_question Does this estate answer at the address it tells everyone to use?
  purpose It did not. /s/<kind>/<id> handled three hand-written kinds -- parcel, capability, note -- and answered every other subject with a page saying that kind was not addressable yet. That page went out as HTTP 200, so four hundred subjects that the index lists and the kit links were all broken, and the status line told every crawler, link checker and this estate's own tests that they resolved. The soft refusal is what hid the real defect: nothing downstream could tell.
  maturity measured
  source_path cmd/BonfyreWebAddress/src/main.c
  consumes AddressPlane
  consumes SubjectIndex
  consumes ObjectGrammar
  consumes ClosureAlgebra
  publishes DerivedView
  internal_calculus one indexed row plus three counts -- the same read the subject index already does per row -- and the shortest address as a label, because an id is a digest for most kinds. Then the ways in and the relation count. Deliberately a shell: the lenses that fill a subject out are separate reads at their own addresses.
  invariant a miss answers 404; a page that explains nothing was found, served with a status saying something was, is the one failure nothing downstream can detect
  invariant every subject the index will name is a subject the address will answer for; the estate must not generate its own broken links
  invariant the kind in the address must be the kind the store holds, or one subject becomes reachable at many addresses and the canonical address stops being canonical
  invariant an identifier is validated, never rewritten; it was passed through a SQL-literal sanitiser that silently dropped every colon, so the id that reached the store was not the id in the URL and nothing said so
  invariant the canonical address is the cheapest semantic read, not the dearest; everything else grows outward from it, and a URL slower than the surfaces projecting it is not the one anyone links to
  cannot_infer an addressed subject -> a permitted one; this says the estate holds it and where it is projected, never that this caller may act on it
  cannot_infer a resolving address -> a complete subject; the page is a shell and says so, and the absence of a lens here is not the absence of the thing
  witness cmd/BonfyreWebAddress/tests/aperture_test.sh holds the offered path from the organ's side: an arbitrary institution offers no identity operation, the identity root offers exactly the identity operations, and authority is re-checked at submit rather than only at render
  witness components/BonfyreControlPlane/tests/subject-address.test.mjs checks every kind the index lists resolves, that a missing subject and a mismatched kind are both 404, that no miss answers 200, that a malformed identifier is refused rather than rewritten, and that the subject address is not slower than the surfaces projecting it
  invariant the address affords, and does not only describe; the operations authored against a subject's kind are resolved onto its own page, so a subject is where the thing can be acted on rather than a description of it
  invariant one reading of the contract table; the page and the aperture endpoint resolve through the same function, because two readings is how they come to afford different things for the same subject and the same session
  invariant an operation withheld for authority is counted out loud rather than hidden, and the count is written into the bounded part of the page -- it was appended after the address list, so the subject with fifty-three ways in filled the buffer first and the page that most needed to say it silently did not
  cannot_infer an offered aperture -> a completed effect; the grammar applies and the actor is admitted, and the native owner re-resolves authority at submit
  invariant the address says what is owed as well as what may be started; an operation a caller may begin and a condition something is waiting on are different questions, and a subject that is actionable and a subject that is blocked are different states
  invariant the conditions are counted, not closed; the canonical address stays the cheapest read on the estate, and running a closure to its fixed point on every subject page would make the door dearer than the rooms it opens onto
  invariant unknown is reported beside unmet and never folded into it, because nobody asked is a different answer from blocked -- the distinction the algebra exists to keep
  invariant which domains apply is authored: closure_domain names the subject kind, the requirement table, the scope column and the satisfied state, so a new domain is a row and the organ names no domain
  known_gap the projection covers no subject today: both declared closure domains scope to identifiers the matrix does not hold -- institution:nightgrid gates every authored contract through nightgrid:write and holds grants for eight accounts, and world:nightgrid-friday-900 carries eight requirements and seven resolvers, and neither was ever bound as a subject
  known_gap the full leverage -- what one more commitment would unlock -- lives behind its own address and is linked rather than resolved here; the door counts and the room computes
  known_gap thirteen kinds resolve through one generic door, so a subject's own grammar contributes nothing to its page; an Event and a BudgetRequest currently read identically apart from their counts

