  source canonical-subject-address
  destination subject-index
  relation reads
  preserves the identity and the counts exactly as the index computes them; the page and the index cannot disagree about a subject
  drops the listing; naming one subject returns that subject rather than its neighbours
  forbidden_inference an addressable subject -> a subject worth showing
  forbidden_inference a count of projections -> access to any of them

architecture compounding-slice
  canonical_name CompoundingSlice
  family surface
  domain Surface / Narrowing
  layer surface
  semantic_question Can a reader narrow what a surface shows, and does the address say what they narrowed to?
  purpose A pattern could filter on exactly one authored column, and when the caller supplied no value it defaulted to the literal string "protocols" -- so a pattern with a selector served one arbitrary slice rather than everything, quietly. One filterable column is also why every surface that wanted to narrow by two things did it in JavaScript after the fact, which is the hand-rolling this layer exists to stop: the address then stopped describing what was on screen, and the narrowing could not be linked, shared, bookmarked or descended from.
  maturity implemented
  source_path cmd/BonfyreWebAddress/src/main.c
  consumes SurfacePattern
  publishes SurfaceIR
  internal_calculus every column a pattern serves is filterable by a query parameter of its own name, and the terms AND together in one prepared statement with each value bound. A value beginning with ~ matches a substring, which is what a column holding a list in one cell needs. A parameter naming a column the pattern does not serve is refused with the list that would have worked.
  invariant only columns the pattern already serves are sliceable; a filter is a narrowing of what is published, never a way to read a column that is not
  invariant every value is bound, never interpolated
  invariant a parameter the pattern cannot honour is refused out loud; a filter that silently matches everything is how a caller comes to believe a narrowing happened when it did not
  invariant a slice narrows and never grants; it cannot raise the extent, reach past the status filter, or return a row the unsliced surface would have withheld
  cannot_infer a sliceable column -> a column worth slicing on; the refusal lists what this pattern serves, never that any value of it exists
  cannot_infer an empty slice -> an empty world; it says no row here matches, not that nothing does
  witness components/BonfyreControlPlane/tests/compounding-slice.test.mjs checks that terms compound, that a substring term reaches a list-valued column, that an unknown field is refused with the sliceable list, that a slice cannot widen what the unsliced surface returns, and that the selector's old hardcoded default is gone
  known_gap the substring form exists because multi-valued columns hold comma-separated strings; the mature form is typed references compiled from friendly authoring, and until then a slice on such a column is a text match that can hit a value the author did not mean
  known_gap facet counts are not returned, so a caller that wants to know which values exist has to fetch the unsliced surface and count them itself

interaction address-offers-authored-operations
  source canonical-subject-address
  destination object-grammar
  relation projects
  preserves the contract's applicability and the authority it requires exactly as authored; the address resolves apertures and decides none of them
  preserves the withholding: an operation this actor cannot perform is omitted and counted, never shown disabled
  drops the atomic form itself; the page links to it rather than rendering it, so the unresolved human variables are asked where the operation is submitted
  forbidden_inference an offered aperture -> a completed effect
  forbidden_inference an operation shown on a subject -> an operation this caller may perform; authority is re-resolved by the owner at submit

interaction address-counts-what-is-owed
  source canonical-subject-address
  destination closure-algebra
  relation reads
  preserves each condition's own state exactly as the domain declared it, including UNKNOWN, which is never folded into unmet
  preserves the dispatch as authored; the address names no domain and learns which apply from closure_domain
  drops the fixed point; the door counts what is outstanding and links to the closure that computes what would unlock it
  forbidden_inference an unmet condition -> a refusal
  forbidden_inference a closed condition -> a condition satisfied in the world

architecture reference-contract
  canonical_name ReferenceContract
  family surface
  domain Surface / Reference integrity
  layer surface
  semantic_question When one authored row names rows in another type, does the name still find anything?
  purpose Eighty-two references in this estate are comma-separated strings an author typed, and every one was resolved at runtime by splitting on commas and dropping whatever did not match. Three readers of one such field had already drifted apart on how they split it. The failure is silent by construction: a world naming a source that was removed renders less and says nothing, which is indistinguishable from a source that answered with nothing. The estate was consistent by care, and nothing made breaking it loud.
  maturity measured
  source_path cmd/BonfyreCMS/content-types/reference_contract.json
  source_path scripts/reference-check.mjs
  source_path scripts/reference_check.py.archive
  source_path site/matrix/surfaces/build/reference-contracts.jsonl
  source_path site/matrix/surfaces/build/install_authored_rows.mjs
  witness site/matrix/surfaces/build/authored_rows.test.mjs checks the five authored reference contracts and local import preflight without remote SSH
  consumes ContentRecord
  publishes DerivedView
  internal_calculus a contract declares the holder type, its key and the field that names things, what the name resolves to -- a row of another type, a column of one, or a table -- the cardinality and separator, whether the reference is required, and what it costs when the name stops resolving. A column target reads its type from a field of the same row, because surface_pattern names its table in one column and that table's columns in another. A holder backed by something other than a table names no columns and is skipped rather than reported broken. The checker declares no type and no field of its own, so a new reference is a row.
  invariant friendly authoring stays friendly; what changes is that the naming is checked once rather than split at runtime by whichever surface happens to read it
  invariant the checker names no type of its own; a checker that knows what a world is means the estate has stopped describing itself
  invariant a declaration names tables and columns and is held to being an identifier, because those names are interpolated into the query the check runs
  invariant a break is loud and specific: the holder and the dead name are both reported, the process exits non-zero, and the report says which thing is missing -- a column that was never in the table and a row that was removed are different repairs
  invariant one broken row does not disable the check for the others; a row naming a table that is gone is dangling, while a declaration that cannot be evaluated at all is undeclarable
  invariant nothing declared does not read as everything resolving; an empty run and a clean run are different answers and must not look alike
  invariant a required reference that is empty is dangling and is counted, not merely listed -- it was appended to the record and never added to the total, so a run reported the break in its listing and still exited clean
  invariant the cost of a break is stated per reference, because they differ: a world losing a source shows less material, and a world naming a grammar no renderer carries hands a reader a link to nowhere
  cannot_infer a resolving reference -> a useful one; the name finds a row, never that the row carries anything worth showing
  cannot_infer a declared reference -> an enforced one at write time; this checks a store as it stands and does not yet stand between an author and a bad name
  witness components/BonfyreControlPlane/tests/reference-contracts.test.mjs runs the Bun checker against a store shaped like the estate's and breaks it: a dangling name exits non-zero and reports both the holder and the dead name, a required-and-empty reference is counted, a declaration naming a missing table is undeclarable rather than clean, a declaration naming something that is not an identifier is refused without running, and the checker names none of this estate's own types
  known_gap the check runs against a store as it stands; nothing stops a bad name being written, so the compile is a gate in CI rather than at the moment of authoring
  known_gap seven references cover two hundred and seventy-one names; the ones still undeclared are pack_demand's needs and preferred_sources, surface_pattern's visible_when and time_field, and territory_presentation's sections, whose targets are sections of a document rather than rows of a type
  known_gap order_by holds a clause rather than a name, so it cannot be checked as a reference and a pattern ordering by a column that is gone still fails at serve time

interaction reference-check-reads-the-declaration
  source reference-contract
  destination content-write-surface
  relation reads
  preserves the authored names exactly; the check resolves them and rewrites nothing
  drops the ordering and the separator once resolved; what survives is whether each name finds a row
  forbidden_inference a resolved name -> a row worth showing
  forbidden_inference a clean check -> a store nothing can break; it says the names resolve now

architecture parcel-projection
  canonical_name ParcelProjection
  family surface
  domain Surface / Carrier projection
  layer surface
  semantic_question Do the dialects of one source agree about which rows they are showing?
  purpose They did not. Three carriers -- OGC Features, RESO Web API and MISMO -- read one projection of the parcel estate, and that projection took its page with LIMIT and no ORDER BY over a UNION ALL of two columnar sources. Observed live: RESO returned account 02145189 where OGC and MISMO returned 00000051, from the same projection, seconds apart. A client paging any carrier could see a row twice or never, and three dialects that cannot agree on the order of the same rows are not projections of one thing.
  maturity measured
  source_path cmd/BonfyreWebAddress/src/main.c
  consumes ForeignTwin
  publishes DerivedView
  internal_calculus each source contributes its own page ordered by observation_id -- the primary key of the hot table and a real column of the cold Parquet, present in both halves of the composed view -- and the union of two pages is small enough to order exactly. The JSON is extracted for the surviving page rather than for the estate, so a page costs a scan of one column and twenty-five parses instead of six and a half million.
  invariant a page is deterministic; the same request returns the same rows, or pagination is a lie and the dialects are free to disagree
  invariant determinism is bought on a stored column, never a parsed one; ordering on account is json_extract_string over the whole estate, measured at twenty seconds a request and enough concurrent memory to take the host off the network
  invariant the ordering key is not served; an internal key projected into a carrier payload is an undeclared field, and a deployment growing one of those is how one meaning becomes three grammars again
  invariant the limit is applied before the extraction, not after; the cost of a page must not scale with the estate
  cannot_infer an agreed page -> agreed meaning; the carriers return the same rows, and what each dialect calls them is the profile's business
  cannot_infer assessment roll data -> an MLS listing, a listing agreement, or an appraisal
  witness components/BonfyreControlPlane/tests/live-carrier-conformance.test.mjs reads all three carriers three times and requires that they agree with each other on every read and that the answer does not move between reads, that the projection orders on observation_id rather than on account, that no carrier serves the ordering key, and that a page is answered in under eight seconds
  known_gap a deterministic page costs about two seconds against under one for the nondeterministic one; the remaining cost is the top-n over six and a half million rows, and a materialised parcel index would remove it
  known_gap only the first page is ordered; there is no cursor, so a caller cannot ask for the next page and the determinism is per request rather than across a walk

interaction carriers-share-one-projection
  source parcel-projection
  destination domain-profile
  relation projects
  preserves the row set and its order identically across every dialect; the carriers differ in what they call a field and never in which rows they are showing
  drops the ordering key at the boundary; it decides the page and is not part of it
  forbidden_inference a carrier answering -> a listing, an agreement or an appraisal
  forbidden_inference agreement between dialects -> agreement about meaning

architecture actor-presentation-cell
  canonical_name ActorPresentationCell
  family surface
  domain Surface / Presentation algebra
  layer surface/declaration
  semantic_question How may one interaction grammar present a resolved closure without turning presentation class into a role or authority hierarchy?
  purpose A finite authored 15 × 4 matrix keyed by interaction grammar and presentation class. The sixty cells state human question, experience character, sections, renderer preference, interaction density, affordance families, external-demand roles, empty/refusal language, extent and navigation. They describe only a projection; AddressPlane, ActorClosure, PackGraph, RelationGraph, Work, Institution and InformationPolicy continue to resolve the facts a cell needs.
  maturity implemented
  source_path schemas/control/surface-presentation-cells.cue
  source_path cmd/BonfyreExternalEstate/src/main.c
  source_path cmd/BonfyreWebAddress/src/main.c
  source_path cmd/BonfyreScopedRespecialize/src/main.c
  source_path cmd/BonfyreScopedRespecialize/tests/scoped_respecialize_test.sh
  consumes SurfaceGrammar
  consumes AddressPlane
  consumes InformationPolicy
  publishes SurfaceIR
  internal_calculus cartesian declaration of fifteen grammars and four presentation classes is compiled by CUE and imported into the native C/SQLite candidate reservoir. WebAddress resolves class from the session closure, passes only declared shape to the read-only organ, and returns a server-rendered fragment. Relation, Pack, institution and work enrichment remain absent until their real owner supplies them.
  invariant every grammar has exactly anonymous, personal, member_worker and operator cells; duplicate or missing cells refuse compilation
  invariant a class changes projection wording, density, renderer preference and offered affordance family, never candidate identity by itself
  invariant a cell contains no actor identity, role, authority, audience entitlement, provider admission, payment authority, spend authority or effect authority
  invariant InformationPolicy still controls disclosure after a cell is selected
  invariant changing presentation class alone never changes candidate identity; the native class token alters wording, density and renderer choice while the public candidate slice remains source-derived material
  invariant a page receives its declared cell through its existing native-served address; a route is enriched in place rather than redirected into a parallel application
  invariant browser session proof is the same-origin HttpOnly session cookie; a generated surface never reads or sends a localStorage bearer as authentication
  cannot_infer personal cell -> a person owns a relation, Habitat or Pack closure
  cannot_infer member_worker cell -> membership, institutional role or work authority
  cannot_infer operator cell -> provider admission, publication, payment, spend or effect authority
  witness cmd/BonfyreExternalEstate/tests/external_estate_test.sh compiles the CUE set, requires exactly sixty imported cells, imports all 4,891 occurrences into SQLite, and proves a native spatial fragment contains no script
  known_gap a native FactDelta respecializer now recomputes an already-resolved subject/scope closure and hands only newly selected external candidates to the existing material lowerer. PackGraph and World still need to expose their complete scoped closures to the SitePresentation reader before a presentation cell may claim personal, institutional or work-specific membership.
  known_gap member_worker/operator selection currently uses active institutional grants as an aggregate presentation signal. A full address-specific RelationGraph, PackGraph and Work closure can enrich a cell only after those owners expose a scoped closure endpoint.

